<article class="env-example" id="09CB">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09CB">Example <span data-tag="09CB">14.34.7</span>.</a> In Example <a href="/tag/08NA" data-tag="08NA">14.34.5</a> we have $X_ n(B) = A[A[\ldots [B]\ldots ]]$ with $n + 1$ brackets. We describe the maps constructed above using a typical element </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xi = \sum \nolimits _ i a_ i [x_{i, 1}] \ldots [x_{i, m_ i}] \in A[A[B]] = X_1(B)  \]
</div>
  <p> where for each $i, j$ we can write </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  x_{i, j} = \sum a_{i, j, k} [b_{i, j, k, 1}] \ldots [b_{i, j, k, n_{i, j, k}}] \in A[B]  \]
</div>
  <p> Obviously this is horrendous! To ease the notation, to see what the $A$-algebra maps $d_0, d_1 : A[A[B]] \to A[B]$ are doing it suffices to see what happens to the variables $[x]$ where </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  x = \sum a_ k [b_{k, 1}] \ldots [b_{k, n_ k}] \in A[B]  \]
</div>
  <p> is a general element. For these we get </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  d_0([x]) = x = \sum a_ k [b_{k, 1}] \ldots [b_{k, n_ k}] \quad \text{and}\quad d_1([x]) = \left[\sum a_ k b_{k, 1} \ldots b_{k, n_ k}\right]  \]
</div>
  <p> The maps $s_0, s_1 : A[A[B]] \to A[A[A[B]]]$ are given by </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  s_0([x]) = \left[\left[\sum a_ k [b_{k, 1}] \ldots [b_{k, n_ k}]\right]\right] \quad \text{and}\quad s_1([x]) = \left[\sum a_ k [[b_{k, 1}]] \ldots [[b_{k, n_ k}]]\right]  \]
</div>
</article>